  "notes": "Conventions. (1) Powertrain: each vehicle is scored for its most common form worldwide today, except for the deliberately historical objects (steam locomotive, horse-drawn carriage, street cable car, sail training tall ship, dog sled), which are scored as they actually operate. 'petrol family car' and 'battery electric car' are listed as separate objects precisely so the combustion/electric split is explicit rather than hidden in a variant judgement; every other object gets one powertrain reading. Vehicles with both bits set are genuinely dual: diesel-electric locomotive, modern cruise ship and conventional (diesel-electric) military submarine all burn fuel to drive electric propulsion motors. Vehicles with neither bit set are propelled by muscle, wind, gravity or an external cable (bicycle, cargo bicycle, horse-drawn carriage, dog sled, kayak, rowed passenger gondola, tall ship, hot air balloon, sailplane, hang glider, railway freight wagon, funicular car, aerial gondola lift, street cable car) \u2014 the cable-hauled and towed vehicles carry no engine of their own even though the system that moves them does. An explicit 'unpowered' attribute was deliberately omitted because on this object set it would be exactly the negation of the two powertrain attributes and would add no lattice structure. (2) Guideway vs road: 'runs on rails or a fixed guideway' covers steel rail, cable and monobeam. Tram and street cable car are marked as travelling on roads as well, because they share the public carriageway; subway, funicular and gondola lift are not. Aerial gondola lift is not marked as flying \u2014 'flies through the air' means aerodynamic or buoyant flight, not being suspended from a cable. Hovercraft and seaplane are genuinely bimodal and carry two medium bits. (3) Licence: true means a legal qualification beyond an ordinary car driving licence \u2014 CDL/HGV, PSV, motorcycle endorsement, forklift certification, gondolier's licence, train driver's certificate, ship's master's ticket, pilot's licence, commercial remote-pilot certificate. Motorhome is true on the >3.5 t class that needs a C1/heavy entitlement in most of Europe. Farm tractor, bulldozer and combine harvester are false because agricultural and plant machinery is generally exempt or covered by the ordinary licence. (4) Purpose: 'carrying people' and 'carrying freight' are both false for vehicles whose job is something else (fire engine, street sweeper, bulldozer, combine harvester, tugboat, military submarine). Pickup truck, car ferry and dog sled carry both bits. A vehicle whose only occupant is its own rider (bicycle, motorcycle, kayak, hang glider) counts as carrying people. Farm tractor is scored as freight because its transport role is hauling implements and trailers. (5) Mass: unladen/empty mass over 1000 kg, a checkable published figure; it separates micro-mobility and light aircraft from road and rail vehicles. Aerial gondola cabin (~0.5 t) is false, funicular car is true. (6) Crew: true when normal safe operation needs two or more people aboard, not when a second seat merely exists. (7) Ownership: true when the typical unit is owned by an individual rather than a transport operator, fleet, municipality or armed force. (8) Density note: 'main purpose is carrying people' sits at 0.67 and 'unladen mass over one tonne' at 0.72 \u2014 above the 0.6 preference but well inside the 0.85 hard bar; pushing them down would have meant padding the object set with near-duplicate freight machinery.",
